podman - for running OCI-based containers in Pods

Podman is a daemonless tool for managing OCI containers and pods. This
build packages the v6 series. Networking runs on Netavark/Aardvark-dns
and Pasta, the database is SQLite, and firewalling goes through
nftables.

This is a from-source build and needs a working Go toolchain plus
podman's runtime helpers to be present first:

  - google-go-lang: podman 6 requires Go >= 1.26 to build; Slackware
    15.0's own /usr/bin/go is 1.16 and cannot be used. This build
    looks for the google-go-lang toolchain under /usr/lib64/go*/go
    and refuses to build if it can't find Go >= 1.26.
  - conmon: OCI container monitor.
  - crun and runc: OCI runtimes; podman defaults to crun if both are
    present, and falls back to runc otherwise.
  - netavark and aardvark-dns: default container network stack and
    its DNS resolver.
  - passt: provides the "pasta" binary used for rootless networking.
  - fuse-overlayfs: rootless overlay storage driver.
  - catatonit: init process used by "podman run --init".
  - qemu and virtiofsd: backend for "podman machine" VMs.

Man pages and the go-md2man tool used to build them are generated
from podman's own vendored copy at build time; no separate go-md2man
package is required.

Default /etc/containers/registries.conf, mounts.conf and policy.json
are installed as .new files and merged on upgrade the usual SBo way.

These container runtimes can run as non-root, but require sub-uid/gid
mappings. To run podman as a user:

    echo 'user.max_user_namespaces=30111' >> /etc/sysctl.conf
    echo '<YOUR_USER>:100000:65536' >> /etc/subuid
    echo '<YOUR_USER>:100000:65536' >> /etc/subgid

KNOWN GAP - "podman machine" does not work out of the box: every
machine provider, qemu included, needs gvproxy (from the
gvisor-tap-vsock project) for VM networking, and gvproxy is not
currently packaged on SBo. "podman machine init" will fail with a
missing-binary error until gvisor-tap-vsock is built and installed
separately; this is not covered by this SlackBuild. Running
containers directly with "podman run"/"podman pod" etc. is unaffected.
